  {
   "id": "concept3",
   "type": "concept",
   "title": {
    "en": "Dorsiventral and isobilateral leaves"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "A dicot leaf is dorsiventral. Its lower (abaxial) epidermis generally has more stomata than the upper (adaxial) one. The mesophyll is differentiated into elongated, vertically arranged palisade parenchyma on the upper side and loosely arranged spongy parenchyma below. A monocot leaf is isobilateral: stomata are on both surfaces and the mesophyll is not differentiated into palisade and spongy layers. In grasses, large, empty, colourless bulliform cells on the upper epidermis lose water under stress, so the leaf curls inwards and reduces water loss.",
    "kn": "ದ್ವಿದಳ ಎಲೆ ಪೃಷ್ಠಾಧರ (ಡಾರ್ಸಿವೆಂಟ್ರಲ್): ಕೆಳಮೇಲ್ಮೈಯಲ್ಲಿ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಪತ್ರರಂಧ್ರ, ಪ್ಯಾಲಿಸೇಡ್ ಮತ್ತು ಸ್ಪಂಜಿನಂತಹ ಪ್ಯಾರೆಂಕೈಮಾ. ಏಕದಳ ಎಲೆ ಸಮದ್ವಿಪಾರ್ಶ್ವ; ಹುಲ್ಲಿನಲ್ಲಿ ಬುಲಿಫಾರ್ಮ್ ಕೋಶಗಳು ಎಲೆ ಸುರುಳಿ ಮಾಡಿ ನೀರಿನ ನಷ್ಟ ತಡೆಯುತ್ತವೆ."
   },
   "points": [
    {
     "en": "Dorsiventral: palisade + spongy; more stomata below"
    },
    {
     "en": "Isobilateral: stomata on both surfaces; mesophyll undifferentiated"
    },
    {
     "en": "Bulliform cells: leaf rolling in grasses"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "A dicot leaf is dorsiventral. Its lower (abaxial) epidermis generally has more stomata than the upper (adaxial) one. The mesophyll is differentiated into elongated, vertically arranged palisade parenchyma on the upper side and loosely arranged spongy parenchyma below.",
